[FINAL-TEXT] === DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: false
faulty_devices: none
diagnosis_text:
I reproduced and checked same-desk connectivity across all office access switches and found no switching fault. On switch11 (where typical paired desks connect), laptop4 (172.18.50.14) ↔ laptop5 (172.18.50.15) ping bidirectionally with 0% loss, and the bridge table shows both access ports in br0, state forwarding (bridge link/brctl: eth1, eth2 UP, LOWER_UP, forwarding). On switch1, desk-neighbor pairs pc4↔pc5 (172.16.10.4/5) and laptop2↔laptop3 (172.16.10.12/13) also pass with 0% loss; switch1’s VLAN topology is consistent (br_vlan10: eth1–6; br_vlan20: eth7), and ports are forwarding. On switch10 and switch5, pc15/pc16 and pc11/pc12 likewise communicate normally. These results indicate the L2 subsystem (bridges/ports/VLAN membership) is healthy and not blocking traffic between co-located endpoints.

Mechanistically, if a switch or VLAN isolation issue existed, ARP/ICMP between neighbors would fail; instead, we see normal L2 forwarding and expected TTLs. Localization checks on the implicated access switches (switch11: eth1/eth2; switch1: eth1–4,7) show no misconfiguration or err-disabled ports.

Root cause: No network-side fault observed; the reported “can’t talk at all” condition is not reproducible on the switching infrastructure.

Corrective fix: None required on the network. Please verify endpoint settings: ensure OS firewalls allow file-sharing protocols and that sharing is enabled. If the two machines are in different subnets (e.g., switch1 VLAN10 vs VLAN20), broadcast-based discovery won’t work—either use direct IP/hostname or request both ports be placed in the same VLAN if policy permits. I’m available to assist with that change if needed. 
=== END ===